  "settingsWhyAllProtections": "All of Zuno's protections",
  "settingsWhyApprovalDeliberate": "Until then, the new device cannot read anything older than itself. That is deliberate: if it were not really you, it would get nothing.",
  "settingsWhyApprovalHeading": "Why new devices ask for approval",
  "settingsWhyApprovalStranger": "When you sign in somewhere new, your other devices have no way to tell it apart from a stranger signing in as you. Approving it, by scanning a code between the two, is how they find out.",
  "settingsWhyConfirmedOnlyHeading": "Confirmed people only",
  "settingsWhyConfirmedOnlyNormally": "Normally, everyone in a chat can read what you send, on every device they have approved. If someone has not set up recovery, all of their devices can.",
  "settingsWhyConfirmedOnlyTurnOn": "Turn on Confirmed people only, and what you send from this device can be read only by people you have confirmed and by your own approved devices. Everyone else sees a message they cannot read. Once you confirm them, they can read what you send after that.",
  "settingsWhyConfirmingHeading": "Why confirming people matters",
  "settingsWhyConfirmingHow": "Confirming someone, in person by scanning or on a call by comparing pictures, checks their details through a channel the server does not control. It only needs doing once per person, even if they change devices later.",
  "settingsWhyConfirmingServer": "Zuno asks the server which devices belong to your friend. A dishonest server could answer with one of its own and read along. Encryption alone does not stop that, because the wrong source was asked.",
  "settingsWhyInterruptsBody": "Almost never. A new sign-in on your own account, and a person whose security details changed after you confirmed them. Both mean something you can act on. Everything else stays out of your way.",
  "settingsWhyInterruptsHeading": "When Zuno interrupts you",
  "settingsWhyIntroEncrypted": "Your messages are encrypted on your device before they are sent, and only the devices in the chat can decrypt them. Not Zuno, and not the server they pass through.",
  "settingsWhyIntroLimits": "Encryption protects what you say. It does not hide who you talk to, and it cannot stop a screenshot or a compromised device.",
  "settingsWhyRecoveryHeading": "Why a recovery code",
  "settingsWhyRecoveryLocked": "The ability to decrypt your messages lives on your devices, not on the server. That is what keeps them private. It also means that if you lose every device you own, your old messages stay locked forever.",
  "settingsWhyRecoveryWay": "A recovery code is the way back. Twelve words, saved somewhere safe. Enter them on a new device and your messages come back.",
  "settingsWhyTitle": "How this works",
  "shareNoChats": "No chats found",
